---
title: Content that outlives the layout
description: Why separating your writing from your presentation makes a website easier to maintain and change.
pubDate: 2026-10-21
category: Content & design
author: Sean Merriam
---

A website's layout will change. The useful ideas in its articles should be able to come along for the ride.

That sounds straightforward until a redesign reveals how much of the content depends on the current presentation. An introduction lives inside a decorative banner. A warning is distinguished only by its background color. A paragraph refers to “the panel on the right,” even though that panel moves below it on a phone.

Separating content from layout is about making those relationships explicit. The writing describes the subject. A small content model describes what each piece means. Components decide how those pieces appear.

### Home

A plain text file can hold both the article and the metadata around it: a title, a description, a publication date, and a category. The layout can then decide how to present those pieces.

- Write the article once.
- Use its title and description in a preview card.
- Render the full content on a reading page.
- Update the design without rewriting the post.

Consider the description beneath a post title. If it exists only as the first paragraph of the article, a listing page has to extract it or maintain a second version. Giving the description its own field makes its purpose clear. The article and its preview can share the same source without depending on each other's markup.

The same reasoning applies to an author name or publication date. Store the underlying information once, then let each context decide whether to show a compact date, a full byline, or no metadata at all.

### Model

A useful field name explains the content rather than its current visual treatment. A field called `summary` can survive a redesign. A field called `grayBoxText` carries an assumption about how that summary will look.

For an article, a small starting model might include:

- **Title:** the name readers use to identify the piece.
- **Description:** a short explanation of what they will learn.
- **Publication date:** a stable date that can be formatted for display.
- **Category:** a consistent way to group related writing.
- **Body:** the article itself, with headings, paragraphs, and other meaningful structure.

There is no need to create a field for every sentence. Most writing belongs in the body. Separate a piece when it has an independent role, needs validation, or appears somewhere else on the site.

### Structure

Markdoc starts with familiar Markdown and adds explicit tags and variables. That gives authors a way to describe structured content without turning every article into a JavaScript program.

A heading expresses a section boundary. A list expresses a set of related items. A blockquote identifies quoted or highlighted material. Those distinctions are useful even before a stylesheet gives them a particular appearance.

> The content should explain the idea. The components should handle how it looks.

For this post, the category comes directly from its frontmatter: **{% $frontmatter.category %}**. The value has a home in the content file, while the page controls its presentation.

More specialized content may deserve a dedicated tag: a warning with a title, an example with a caption, or a comparison with clearly identified options. Introduce that structure when it captures something meaningful. A tag that merely requests a color or a margin can bring the same layout dependency back under a different name.

### Reuse

Keeping content in one place does not mean every presentation should be identical. A homepage card needs a title, a short description, and a destination. A reading page needs the complete argument and enough context to make sense on its own.

Use shared fields for the facts those contexts have in common. Avoid squeezing the entire article into the preview or expecting a truncated paragraph to function as a useful summary.

Some content should remain local. A paragraph explaining a particular example may never need to appear elsewhere. Extracting it into a reusable object would make editing more complicated without improving the reader's experience.

A helpful question is: if this information changes, would someone need to update it in more than one place? If the answer is yes, a shared field or reference may help. If the answer is no, ordinary prose may be the better home.

### Workflow

The right setup is one that the people maintaining the site can use comfortably. Start with a small content model, give each field a clear purpose, and add complexity only when the writing calls for it.

Validation can catch a missing title or an invalid date, but the error needs to help the author act. A preview matters just as much: people should be able to see how a long title wraps, where an image appears, and whether a heading actually improves the article.

Write down the small decisions that are easy to forget. Explain where images go, what belongs in a description, and how categories are chosen. A short example file is often more useful than a large collection of abstract rules.

### Redesign

A practical way to evaluate the separation is to imagine replacing the current layout. Could the article still be read in a simple document? Would its headings make sense? Could the preview be rebuilt from the existing fields?

If the answers are mostly yes, the content has a reasonable degree of independence. The next design can change the typography, spacing, and component arrangement without forcing a rewrite of the underlying ideas.

That is the useful payoff: a site whose appearance can evolve while its writing remains understandable, editable, and worth keeping.
